{
  "text": "The skin of late‑stage embryos, larvae, and young post‑metamorphic amphibians was examined with a focus on how cells stick together. Using high‑resolution imaging and special stains for material outside the cells, the detailed organization of the cell‑to‑cell and cell‑to‑basement‑membrane attachment sites and their associated internal fiber networks were studied. No continuous bridges of internal fibers were seen linking neighboring cells at the junctions. Instead, most fibers that approach these sites turn toward a dense plaque, loop either within or around it, and then re‑enter the main fiber bundles of the cell, suggesting that the internal framework mainly bears tension while adhesion is provided by extracellular material that builds up at the contact points. This external material appears as pillar‑like or partitioned structures that merge with or sit on the outer leaflets of the cell membrane and form a discontinuous dense line in the middle of the junctions. A similar pattern was observed for the basal attachment sites. Between the basal cell edge and the underlying basement membrane, a globular layer composed of lipids and sugar‑protein complexes was found, whose role is still unclear. These findings support the idea that many types of adhesive specializations exist, with the larger, more organized junctions acting as strong, well‑supported attachment points that complement a more general aggregating mechanism."
